<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sverre of Norway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1150–1202</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Sverre Sigurdsson, Sverre Sigurdsson of Norway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sverre Sigurdsson was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1184 to 1202, having led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiner</a> faction in the civil wars from 1177. Claiming to be a son of King Sigurd Munn, he defeated his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlingsson">Magnus Erlingsson</a> and strengthened royal power against the church, which excommunicated him. His career is told in Sverris saga, written partly under his own direction.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sverre-of-norway.jpg" alt="Sverre of Norway" width="222" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Bugge (1870–1929) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sverre_Sigurdsson_(Nidaros_Cathedral).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
